# SLAGBALL — recovered script.
#
# SLAGBALL, a Sub-Channel 2½ Saturday comedy. The Sump, a beloved dive with a pit in
# the back and forty thousand credits of debt, enters the Coldwater Slagball Open
# against MAXIM VIGO’s corporate juggernaut APEX PRIME. A washed-up coach, a pirate, a
# fainting boy, a silent giant, a lethal waitress, a doomed romantic, and a lawyer who
# forgot which side she was on: one bracket, one sudden-dark overtime, one clause
# nobody read. A true underdog story, thrown in the dark.
#
# This film predated the .bsm pipeline: it existed only as a compiled graph in
# content. This script was decompiled from that graph and verified by RECOMPILING
# it and diffing node-for-node against the original, so it is the same picture,
# not a rewrite. Edit here from now on — content is the build output, not the source.
#
# A film is a RECORDING: the cast are DISPLAY NAMES, never npc_ ids, so nothing
# here spawns a studio NPC and nothing presence-gates. See docs/bsm-format.md.

::cast
DUTCH | Dutch | never won a thing until it counted
LOTTIE | Lottie | minded the beam
VESS | Vess | read clause nine
MAXIM VIGO | Maxim Vigo | pre-lost
GILES | Giles | the universe is a slag; he is the pit
PIP | Pip | won a tournament asleep
MERV | Merv | "Yeah."
BARNACLE BASH | Barnacle Bash | found the water within
COACH RUSK | Coach Rusk | got out of the fern
BLURT | Blurt | 
CASS | Cass | 
THE REF | The Ref | 
::endcast

TITLE slagball_title

SLUG SLAGBALL | a Sub-Channel 2½ presentation

MUSIC before_the_light
♪ A triumphant sports-anthem synth that is one keyboard preset away from a funeral, and knows it. ♪
MUSIC_END

SHOT
A held shot: a neon pit in a bad part of Coldwater. Two teams. A crate of foam-wrapped slag orbs. And a great deal of poor judgment.
SHOT_END

NARRATOR:
This is Slagball. The rules are simple. You throw a slag. You hit the other side. They sit down.

NARRATOR:
If they catch it, YOU sit down, and you think about the choices that brought you here.

NARRATOR:
It was invented by bored dock hands who ran out of things to resent. It is the second-most popular sport in the basin.

NARRATOR:
The most popular sport in the basin is watching people who are worse off than you. Slagball is often both at once.

WAIT 2

SHOT
THE SUMP. A bar with a pit in the back, held together by grease, sentiment, and one very tired support beam.
SHOT_END

SHOT
Its owner, DUTCH, is a large kind man who has never won anything, including arguments with his own suppliers.
SHOT_END

DUTCH:
Morning, Lottie. What’s the damage.

LOTTIE:
The tap’s out, the drone repossessed a stool while you were asleep, and a man from a bank is standing in your pit.

DUTCH:
We don’t have a man from a bank.

LOTTIE:
We do now. He brought a clipboard. Nobody brings a clipboard to say something nice.

SHOT
In the pit stands VESS: sharp, pressed, holding a clipboard like a weapon she is licensed to carry.
SHOT_END

VESS:
Are you Dutch? The Dutch who owns this... establishment?

DUTCH:
Depends who’s asking and how much they’re asking for.

VESS:
I’m asking for forty thousand credits, and I’m asking today, and my name is Vess.

DUTCH:
Well, Vess, I’ve got about forty credits and a lot of what I’d call ambiance.

VESS:
The bank calls it a fire hazard with a liquor license.

LOTTIE:
She’s not wrong, Dutch.

DUTCH:
Lottie, whose side are you on.

LOTTIE:
The side that still has a roof. I’m flexible about which side that is.

SHOT
Vess produces a stylus and begins itemizing the Sump the way a coroner itemizes a body.
SHOT_END

VESS:
One bar, structurally optimistic. One pit, purpose unclear. Fourteen stools, one recently repossessed. And a smell I’m choosing not to catalogue for legal reasons.

DUTCH:
That smell is character.

VESS:
Character doesn’t pay a lien, Dutch. I’ve tried. I once billed a man’s personality. It bounced.

DUTCH:
You bill personalities?

VESS:
I bill everything. It’s the only language the bank speaks and I’m fluent and I hate it.

SHOT
A regular at the end of the bar quietly finishes his drink, sets down exactly no credits, and leaves. Dutch watches him go with the fondness of a man being robbed by family.
SHOT_END

DUTCH:
That’s Old Pemms. He hasn’t paid since the Waking. He’s basically furniture.

LOTTIE:
Furniture we can’t repossess because he’s technically alive. We checked. Twice. Hopefully.

VESS:
This is the worst-run business I have ever been sent to close, and I closed a clinic that was legally a boat.

DUTCH:
And yet you’re still standing in my pit.

VESS:
The exit’s behind Old Pemms and I’m not ready to have that conversation.

WAIT 2

SLUG MEANWHILE, ACROSS TOWN | the APEX Optimization Spire

MUSIC before_the_light
♪ A chrome corporate fanfare, the kind that plays in an elevator that thinks very highly of itself. ♪
MUSIC_END

SHOT
APEX. A fitness-arena empire so glossy you can see your own inadequacy in the floor. At its summit: MAXIM VIGO.
SHOT_END

SHOT
VIGO is speaking. VIGO is always speaking. Currently he is speaking to a hologram of himself, which is the only audience that has never let him down.
SHOT_END

MAXIM VIGO:
Look at us. Look at what we’ve optimized. There is not one soft thing left in this entire tower. Not one.

MAXIM VIGO:
Softness is just strength that gave up early. I read that. I think I said it. I’ll have someone check who said it and then I’ll have said it.

SHOT
An assistant approaches, terrified in the specific way of people paid well to be terrified.
SHOT_END

MAXIM VIGO:
Tell me something optimized.

VESS:
Sir, the Sump acquisition. The owner can’t pay. Foreclosure clears in thirty days. The whole block is ours.

MAXIM VIGO:
The Sump. That grease-stain with a pit. I want it flattened and turned into an APEX Wellness Annex.

MAXIM VIGO:
Do you know what people need, Vess? People need to be told they are almost good enough, forever, for a monthly fee.

VESS:
That’s... genuinely the darkest thing I’ve heard in a boardroom, and I do foreclosures.

MAXIM VIGO:
Thank you. Put it on a banner.

SHOT
VIGO admires his own jaw in the window, which is a thing his jaw is very used to.
SHOT_END

MAXIM VIGO:
Nobody has ever beaten me at anything. Do you know why? Because I only enter things I have already bought.

MAXIM VIGO:
That’s not cheating. That’s pre-winning. Legal team loves it. Put THAT on a banner too. I’m on fire today.

SHOT
The hologram of Vigo nods along, because it is programmed to, and because it, too, has never been told no.
SHOT_END

MAXIM VIGO:
You agree. Of course you agree. You’re the only one in this building with taste, and you’re me.

VESS:
Sir, should I be worried you only take counsel from a projection of yourself?

MAXIM VIGO:
Worried? Vess, it’s the most efficient relationship I’ve ever had. He never asks for a raise and he agrees with me at the speed of light. Literally. He’s light.

VESS:
That’s... I’m going to write "concerning" in my notes and move on.

MAXIM VIGO:
Write "visionary." Cross out "concerning." Actually, fire whoever taught you the word "concerning." It’s soft. It’s a soft word.

SHOT
Vigo strides to a wall of screens showing every APEX arena in the basin, all identical, all optimized, all empty of anything a person would call joy.
SHOT_END

MAXIM VIGO:
Look at it. A hundred arenas. Not one of them has a pit. Do you know why? Because a pit is where the poor go to feel like they matter, and mattering is not a subscription tier.

VESS:
You could add a mattering tier.

MAXIM VIGO:
Don’t tempt me with a good idea before lunch, Vess. Flatten the Sump. I want the whole basin optimized before I have to feel anything about any of it.

WAIT 2

SLUG BACK AT THE SUMP | thirty days on the clock

SHOT
Vess returns to the Sump to deliver the papers. She does not expect to feel anything. This is, professionally, the plan.
SHOT_END

VESS:
Thirty days, Dutch. Raise forty thousand or the block goes to APEX and this pit becomes a place where people feel bad about their cores.

DUTCH:
Forty thousand. I could sell everything I own and get to about nine.

VESS:
Then I’d stop at nine. Save yourself the everything.

SHOT
A regular, GILES, looks up from the bar, heartbroken already, which is his natural resting state.
SHOT_END

GILES:
They can’t take the Sump. I met the love of my life here.

LOTTIE:
Giles, you met the love of your life here eleven times. Three of them left through that window.

GILES:
And I’d wait for all of them to come back. That’s what the Sump IS, Lottie. It’s a place you come back to.

SHOT
It is, briefly, moving. Then a support beam groans, and everyone flinches on instinct.
SHOT_END

DUTCH:
Vess. Off the record. Is there any way, any way at all, that a place like this beats a thing like APEX?

VESS:
Off the record? No. On the record, also no, but with a stamp.

VESS:
You’d need a miracle, a lawyer, and forty thousand credits, and you have a beam that’s actively quitting.

DUTCH:
What if I told you I could get you the lawyer for free.

VESS:
I’d say you don’t have that kind of charm.

DUTCH:
No. But the Sump does. Everybody who works here started as a customer who couldn’t afford to leave.

SHOT
Vess looks at the papers. Vess looks at the pit. Something professional in her sets down its clipboard, just for a second.
SHOT_END

VESS:
Get me a reason. A real one. And I’ll pretend I forgot which side I’m on for exactly as long as it’s interesting.

GILES:
I have a reason. Her name was Marisol. Or Deb. It was one of the two and they both left through the window.

LOTTIE:
Giles, nobody is asking about the window women right now.

GILES:
Somebody should. Somebody always should. That’s the tragedy of the Sump, Lottie: too many windows, not enough staying.

VESS:
Is he always like this?

DUTCH:
Only when he’s awake. Asleep he’s worse. He narrates.

SHOT
Old Pemms, still furniture, raises a finger as though to contribute, thinks better of it, and lowers it. It is the most anyone has ever heard from Old Pemms.
SHOT_END

DUTCH:
See that? Even Pemms cares. Pemms hasn’t cared about anything since they took the sun private.

VESS:
That is the lowest bar for hope I have ever been asked to clear, and yet.

DUTCH:
That’s the Sump’s whole business model, Vess. The lowest bar, cleared by people who had no business jumping.

WAIT 2

SLUG THE IDEA | possibly a bad one

MUSIC before_the_light
♪ A tiny hopeful theme, played on what is clearly a broken keyboard, which somehow helps. ♪
MUSIC_END

SHOT
That night, PIP, the anxious kid who buses the tables, is watching a flyer flicker on the wall.
SHOT_END

PIP:
Dutch? Dutch, um. This is probably nothing. It’s definitely nothing. I’ll go.

DUTCH:
Pip. Breathe. What is it.

PIP:
The Coldwater Slagball Open. Grand prize is... it’s forty thousand credits. It’s exactly forty thousand credits.

SHOT
The whole bar turns and looks at the flyer as if it had personally arrived to save them, which, narratively, it has.
SHOT_END

LOTTIE:
That’s not a coincidence. That’s a screenwriter.

DUTCH:
We enter a Slagball tournament. We win exactly what we owe. We keep the Sump.

VESS:
Do any of you play Slagball?

SHOT
A long silence. Somewhere, the tired support beam creaks, as if to answer for them.
SHOT_END

DUTCH:
We own a Slagball pit.

VESS:
Owning a pit is not playing Slagball. I own a treadmill. I am not a horse.

GILES:
I’ve always wanted to be part of a team. A team is just a group of people who agree to be disappointed together.

LOTTIE:
Giles, that’s a family. A team’s the one where you win sometimes.

DUTCH:
Then we win sometimes. Starting now. Who’s in.

SHOT
Hands go up. Slowly. Reluctantly. The way hands go up when they know the rest of the body disagrees.
SHOT_END

PIP:
I’ll... I’ll do the thing. The team thing. Please don’t make me talk to the crowd.

DUTCH:
Pip, you’re in. Merv?

SHOT
MERV, an enormous man who has said nine words this year, considers the question with great seriousness.
SHOT_END

MERV:
Fine.

DUTCH:
That’s five words. He’s excited.

LOTTIE:
I’m in. I’ve been throwing things at men in this bar for twenty years without a trophy to show for it. Time it counted.

DUTCH:
Lottie, you throw glasses.

LOTTIE:
And I’ve never missed. You do the math on who your best player is and then apologize to me for the wage.

GILES:
I’m in too. If the Sump goes down, I want to have loved it out loud at least once, in a uniform, on a team.

DUTCH:
Giles, you understand people will be throwing things at you.

GILES:
Dutch. People have been throwing things at me my whole life. At least in the pit it’ll finally be regulation.

VESS:
That is either the saddest or the healthiest thing I’ve heard all week and I genuinely cannot tell which.

DUTCH:
At the Sump those are the same thing. You’ll get used to it. Or you’ll leave through the window, and we’ll name a stool after you.

PIP:
Do we... do we get uniforms? I don’t want a number that draws attention. Can I be a low number? A shy number?

DUTCH:
Pip, you can be whatever number won’t make you faint. We’ll workshop it.

WAIT 2

SLUG THE TEAM | such as it is

SHOT
A team needs six. The Sump has five and a half, if you count Giles as a whole person, which the Sump generously does.
SHOT_END

SHOT
The sixth arrives on his own. He always does. His name, he insists, is BARNACLE BASH.
SHOT_END

BARNACLE BASH:
Ahoy the pit! I heard there be a crew forming, and a crew be needing a man who fears no water!

LOTTIE:
Bash. There’s no water. There’s never been water. It’s a bar.

BARNACLE BASH:
That be exactly what the sea WANTS ye to think, Lottie.

DUTCH:
Bash, are you actually a pirate?

BARNACLE BASH:
I be as much a pirate as this be a ship, captain. Which is to say: completely, and only me.

PIP:
I think he’s the most confident person I’ve ever met and I find it deeply calming.

BARNACLE BASH:
The boy has SEA SENSE! Ye there, cabin boy, stick by ol’ Bash and ye’ll never drown on dry land, which be the most common way to drown in this city!

PIP:
That’s... genuinely reassuring? I don’t know why. I feel safer. Is that wrong?

LOTTIE:
It’s wrong, but it’s the good kind of wrong. Bash has never once been right and never once been scared. There’s a lesson in there if you don’t look at it directly.

BARNACLE BASH:
Aye! Fear be a fog, and a good captain simply declines to sail through it! I declined years ago! Look at me now: landlocked, beloved, and TERRIFYING at slagball!

DUTCH:
He’s not wrong about the terrifying part. I’ve seen him throw. Grown collectors have crossed the street.

DUTCH:
He throws harder than anyone in the block. I stopped asking questions the day he called the ice machine a kraken and won.

SHOT
Vess reviews the roster with the expression of a woman watching a bridge get built out of chairs.
SHOT_END

VESS:
A frightened boy. A silent mountain. A waitress with a grudge. A romantic with a concussion history. A pirate. And you.

DUTCH:
And you.

VESS:
I’m the LAWYER. I hold the clipboard. I do not enter the pit.

DUTCH:
The rules say six players on the roster, minimum, or you’re disqualified. We have six. If one of us so much as sneezes, we forfeit.

VESS:
So I’m the sneeze insurance.

DUTCH:
You’re the seventh. The one who catches us when we fall. Legally. And, I’m hoping, a little bit in general.

VESS:
Don’t. I have a clipboard and I’m not afraid to file it.

SHOT
But she signs the roster. In the box marked RESERVE she writes her name, and underlines it, and pretends the underline was an accident.
SHOT_END

BARNACLE BASH:
A fine crew! But a crew needs a NAME, captain. Every vessel that ever sailed had a name to curse when she sank!

DUTCH:
We’re the Sump.

BARNACLE BASH:
That be a berth, not a name! We need somethin’ to strike fear! The Drowned Fists! The Kraken’s Regret!

LOTTIE:
The Kraken’s Regret is what I call the men’s room after darts night.

PIP:
What about, and this is dumb, what about "The Sump"? Because that’s what we are and nobody expects the thing that’s just true.

SHOT
A silence. It is, everyone realizes, perfect. The most dangerous thing a Coldwater team can be is exactly what it says on the door.
SHOT_END

DUTCH:
The Sump it is. Pip named the team. Pip, you’ve done one confident thing and I’m very proud.

PIP:
I’m already regretting it. Can we go back to the fear one?

MERV:
No.

DUTCH:
Merv likes it. That settles it. When Merv talks, the ceiling listens.

WAIT 2

SLUG THE COACH | a legend, technically

MUSIC before_the_light
♪ A grizzled old-champion theme, mostly low brass, like a war movie about a man who lost. ♪
MUSIC_END

SHOT
You cannot win the Open without a coach. Every good coach in Coldwater is expensive. So the Sump finds a bad one who used to be great.
SHOT_END

SHOT
COACH RUSK. Once the finest slagger in the basin. Now a wreck on a busted hover-chair that lists permanently to port.
SHOT_END

COACH RUSK:
Get out. I don’t coach. I retired. I sit here and I resent the ceiling. It’s a full schedule.

DUTCH:
They say you never lost a match.

COACH RUSK:
They say a lot of things. They also say this chair is fixable, and look at me listing into the fern.

DUTCH:
We need a coach. We’ve got thirty days, no skill, and a beam that’s given up.

COACH RUSK:
Then you don’t need a coach. You need a priest and a good lawyer.

VESS:
She’s here. She’s expensive. Coach us and she stays free, which is the only free thing in this economy.

SHOT
Rusk looks at the six of them. His eyes, ancient and mean, do a thing they have not done in years: they light up, out of pure contempt.
SHOT_END

COACH RUSK:
You’re all terrible. I can see it from here. The big one’s got no killer instinct, the kid flinches at his own shadow, and the pirate’s a lawsuit.

BARNACLE BASH:
A COMPLIMENT!

COACH RUSK:
It was not.

COACH RUSK:
But terrible I can work with. Terrible’s just great that hasn’t been screamed at yet. Champions I can’t fix. You? You’re all wet clay and low standards. Perfect.

DUTCH:
Is that a yes?

COACH RUSK:
It’s a maybe with the volume of a yes. Wheel me to the pit. And somebody get me out of this fern, I’ve been in it since the spring.

SHOT
They extract Coach Rusk from the fern. Bits of fern come with him. He does not remove them for the rest of the picture. It becomes, in time, a look.
SHOT_END

PIP:
Sir, is it true you once won a match with a broken arm?

COACH RUSK:
Both arms, boy. And a grudge. The grudge did most of it. Never underestimate a well-kept grudge; it’s the only thing in Coldwater that appreciates in value.

GILES:
Coach, what happened? You were the greatest. What takes a legend down to a fern?

COACH RUSK:
Same thing that takes everybody down, romantic. I won everything, and then I had nothing left to be angry at, and a man who runs on anger can’t coast. So I sat down. In a fern, as it turns out.

DUTCH:
So what changed today?

COACH RUSK:
You walked in with six of the worst players I’ve ever seen and asked me to save a bar. That’s the first thing worth being angry about in nine years. Thank you. I hate it. Let’s go.

BARNACLE BASH:
A captain who runs on fury! I’ll follow ye into any storm, ye landlocked terror!

COACH RUSK:
The pirate’s going to be a problem and I’ve decided he’s my favorite. Wheel. Pit. Now.

WAIT 2

SLUG TRAINING | day one of thirty

MUSIC before_the_light
♪ A montage synth, aspirational and cheap, the sound of a man believing in himself against the advice of everyone. ♪
MUSIC_END

SHOT
Rusk’s methods are unorthodox. By which the survivors mean cruel. By which Rusk means effective.
SHOT_END

COACH RUSK:
Rule one. In the pit, everything is trying to hit you. The slag. The crowd. Your own doubt. Your own mother, if she’s in the stands, and mine always was.

COACH RUSK:
So you learn to not be where the throw is going. That’s the whole game. Not being there. I built a career on not being there. Ask my kids.

SHOT
He gestures. A battered repossession drone, UNIT 7, the one that took the stool, hovers in, reprogrammed and furious.
SHOT_END

COACH RUSK:
This is Unit 7. It used to take furniture. Now it takes SLAGS, and it throws them at your soft little heads. Dodge, or learn.

PIP:
That’s a repossession drone. That is a WEAPON.

COACH RUSK:
If you can dodge a repo-drone, boy, you can dodge a slag. And if you can’t dodge a repo-drone, you were going to lose your couch anyway.

SHOT
Unit 7 opens fire. Pip is struck immediately, in the face, with great enthusiasm.
SHOT_END

PIP:
I felt that in my ancestors.

COACH RUSK:
Good. Pain’s just the drone teaching. Free lessons. In this economy you take the free ones. Again.

SHOT
MERV steps up. Unit 7 fires. Merv does not dodge. Merv catches the slag one-handed and crushes it, and the drone, out of respect, backs up.
SHOT_END

COACH RUSK:
Now HIM I don’t have to teach. Him I have to aim.

MERV:
Okay.

SHOT
LOTTIE takes the pit. She has spent twenty years dodging drunks, pinches, and thrown glasses. The drone stands no chance and seems to know it.
SHOT_END

LOTTIE:
Sweetheart, I’ve dodged worse than you on a slow Tuesday.

COACH RUSK:
The waitress is our best player and she started this morning. That’s not a good sign about the rest of you. That’s a GREAT sign about her.

SHOT
GILES, meanwhile, is hit by a slag Unit 7 did not throw. It is unclear where it came from. It happens to Giles a lot.
SHOT_END

GILES:
Coach, I think the universe is a slag and I am the pit.

COACH RUSK:
Finally. The romantic gets it. That’s the only true thing anyone’s said in this dump all week.

SHOT
Rusk introduces the second drill. He calls it "Trust." It involves standing very still while Merv throws a slag as hard as he can at a plank six inches from your head.
SHOT_END

COACH RUSK:
The plank is the lesson, Pip. You flinch, you lose. You trust the drill, you learn where you end and the throw begins. That line’s the whole sport.

PIP:
The plank has a hole in it shaped exactly like a slag.

COACH RUSK:
That’s Merv’s work. He’s never missed the plank. He’s also never hit the plank on purpose. He simply asks it to move and it declines. Watch.

SHOT
Merv throws. The slag goes through the existing hole in the plank without touching the sides, and Pip does not flinch, because Pip has fainted, which Rusk marks down as progress.
SHOT_END

COACH RUSK:
Fainting’s just flinching that committed. We can work with commitment. Wake him up and do it again.

BARNACLE BASH:
COACH! Teach me to throw so hard the SEA apologizes!

COACH RUSK:
Bash, you already throw too hard. Your problem isn’t power, it’s that you announce every throw with a nautical war crime. The other team hears you coming from the docks.

BARNACLE BASH:
The announcin’ is HALF the throw, coach!

COACH RUSK:
It’s a third of the throw and all of the lawsuit. Fine. Keep the shanty. We’ll weaponize the confusion. Nobody’s ever game-planned for a man who thinks the ice machine is a kraken.

WAIT 2

SHOT
Days pass. Bruises bloom and fade. The six of them are, against all decency, getting less terrible.
SHOT_END

COACH RUSK:
You’re still bad. But you’re bad on PURPOSE now. That’s technique. Bad on purpose is a plan. Bad by accident is just Tuesday.

DUTCH:
Coach. Do you think we can actually win this thing?

COACH RUSK:
No.

DUTCH:
Oh.

COACH RUSK:
But I didn’t think I’d get out of that fern either, and here I am, listing at you with hope. Wheel me closer, I’m being sincere and I hate it.

SHOT
One last drill, the night before the Open. Rusk lines all six of them along the pit and turns off the neon, so they train the way champions die: in the dark.
SHOT_END

COACH RUSK:
The eyes lie. In a real match the crowd, the lights, the money on the board, all of it’s screaming at your eyes. So we learn to play without them tonight.

PIP:
How do we throw at something we can’t see?

COACH RUSK:
You listen for the thing that’s sure it can’t lose. Certainty’s loud, kid. Rich certainty is the loudest thing in any room. You aim at the smug and you rarely miss.

SHOT
In the dark, Merv catches a slag he could not possibly have seen, out of pure enormous instinct, and hands it back into the black like a gift.
SHOT_END

MERV:
Easy.

COACH RUSK:
The mountain speaks and the lesson’s over. Everybody drink water. Not you, Bash, you’ll say something.

BARNACLE BASH:
The sea is water and I AM the sea, coach!

COACH RUSK:
And there it is. Bed. All of you. Tomorrow we find out if terrible-on-purpose is a plan or an obituary.

SHOT
Before they go, Rusk keeps Dutch back a moment in the dark pit.
SHOT_END

COACH RUSK:
You know why I never lost, Dutch? Not talent. There were more talented sluggers than me. I never lost because I never once thought I deserved to win. Kept me hungry. Kept me watching.

DUTCH:
That sounds miserable, Coach.

COACH RUSK:
It was. But your lot’s got the same disease and they don’t even know it. Not one of them thinks they deserve a thing. That’s why they might just take everything. The world never sees the undeserving coming.

DUTCH:
